reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:47:1. Report every request, candidate, transport failure, cost and baseline variability. Quantify disagreements C vs T and change in wrong merges, missed repeats, revisions and target group, by request, comparing the three draws in each arm. Score-sensitivity is not proof of accuracy. No search recall improvement can be attributed to this intervention because search is held fixed.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:49:3. The original fresh run returned 3 supersedes answers attached to repeat outcomes; it did not exercise a standalone revision outcome. One selected case has a supersedes decision, so describe it as an individual diagnostic, not revision performance. A separate case set of documented real revisions may be read and assessed; it cannot stand in for the full product path.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:58:Hypothesis: score information moves at least one relevant comparison and makes at least one source-backed decision better without a source-backed decision getting worse. Prediction: the clearly wrong X22 grouping becomes separate more often in T than C, while the selected accepted-repeat controls remain correctly grouped. We do not predict a perfect result. Predeclare individual decision comparisons, 3 samples per arm and show all 66 outcomes and errors. For each outcome and selected source-backed candidate, count correct and incorrect choices across three draws per arm. A repeatable improvement requires at least one agreed previously incorrect decision to become correct in at least 2 of 3 T draws and fewer than 2 of 3 C draws, with no agreed previously correct decision becoming wrong in at least 2 of 3 T draws while correct in at least 2 of 3 C draws. A repeatable regression stops the line. If neither occurs or independent readers disagree, report inconclusive. This is a screening rule for selected examples, not a quantitative estimate of product impact. Blind-read all eleven incoming records against all their candidate sets and source articles, including unchanged controls; report gaps. Register case ids and score mapping and historical top-ten replay before any send; send no paid calls until council review and preflight pass.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:62:The read-only Codex reviewer cleared harness design but not sending. Claude reviewed the earlier 10-case/three-arm draft and found lack of revision coverage, unsupported X22 direction and number presentation ambiguity; an eleventh recorded revision-bearing case was added. Its conclusion that 40 borderline probabilities bound opportunity is a hypothesis; confident outputs can also be wrong. The X22 prediction is a falsifiable probe hypothesis, not evidence of likely improvement. The shuffled arm is removed due to false attribution; C versus T assesses the total intervention, not score causation.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:68:Readers independently classify each new-versus-old statement as same / revision / different / overlapping / unable to decide, using both source articles. Candidate order is shuffled in the reading packet. A multiple-target repeat is acceptable if any source-supported candidate is selected; a revision link must itself be supported; a missing supported revision and a wrong extra revision are errors separately from grouping. Overlap or absent source makes that pair unresolved; it cannot establish improvement or regression. Resolve disagreements by a third reader, and report unresolved without counting correct or wrong. Each of the six draws per case is scored against the same frozen blinded judgments; evaluate repeat target and revision links, not only outcome kind. If model failures or blind review coverage prevent a comparison, report incomplete, never success.
